# "The Significance of the Harmon Farm and the Springs Hotel Woods" (National Park Service study of March 1, 1991): second reading of PDF pp. 32-33 (the burning of the farm and the tenants' claims), workstream bt-web, 2026-11-15.
# The first reading took 11 passages (the farm in 1841 and 1862, the woods, the Herr claim, the Elliott map and the brick house). These passages add the study's narrative of the farm after the burning and the claims of the tenants (Finfruck and Comfort) for crops and livestock. The study cites the Gettysburg Compiler of 1915 and the claim files; its own sentences are quoted. The PDF pages were not looked at as images (the numbers are written in words).
@ NPS-HARMON-FARM
%basis the Gettysburg Compiler of 3 July 1915 and the claim files of the tenants, as the study names them

32 | buildings | The Harmon farm after the burning: the barn foundations above smoldering framing; the brick walls of the house standing although the contents, windows and roof burned | - | 1863-07 (after the burning of the farm) | stated | The study says that after the battle the barn foundations jutted above the smoldering framing and contents and the brick walls of the house remained, although the contents, windows and roof of the house had fallen victim to the flames | #s27-28
33 | fields | The tenant Finfruck claimed ten acres of wheat and five acres of rye lost to the armies' movement and a potato patch and truck garden near the house destroyed | - | 1863-07 (claim of the tenant) | stated | The study says the tenant Finfruck claimed the loss of ten acres of wheat and five acres of rye by the movement of both armies across his crop fields and that a potato patch and truck garden near the house was also destroyed | #s7-8
